<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="vendor/autoload.php"
         colors="true"
         cacheDirectory=".phpunit.cache"
         executionOrder="depends,defects"
         failOnRisky="true"
         failOnWarning="true"
         failOnDeprecation="true"
         beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests="true"
         displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerDeprecations="true"
         displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerErrors="true"
         displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerWarnings="true"
         displayDetailsOnPhpunitDeprecations="true">

    <!--
        SIX SUITES, and the split is not cosmetic — each one has a different database strategy and a
        different set of things it is allowed to fake (see tests/Pest.php):

          Unit         no framework boot at all: no container, no database, no facades. If a unit
                       test needs one of those, it is a Feature test.
          Feature      HTTP through the full stack, RefreshDatabase (one BEGIN/ROLLBACK per test).
          Contract     Laravel<->FastAPI against the OpenAPI document in packages/contracts/.
                       Http::fake() is BANNED here: a faked SSE body is a string, so the relay
                       drains it in microseconds and every buffering, ordering, heartbeat and
                       disconnect bug passes.
          Security     the §22.5 suite. Always runs; never accelerated by test-impact analysis,
                       which follows PHP call graphs and cannot see across the process boundary into
                       FastAPI and Qdrant.
          Integration  real containers, cross-process. The ONLY suite on DatabaseTruncation, because
                       it is the only one where another process must be able to SEE the rows.
          Arch         the doctrine no runtime test can observe. Always runs.
    -->
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="Unit">
            <directory>tests/Unit</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="Feature">
            <directory>tests/Feature</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="Contract">
            <directory>tests/Contract</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="Security">
            <directory>tests/Security</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="Integration">
            <directory>tests/Integration</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="Arch">
            <directory>tests/Arch</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>

    <source ignoreIndirectDeprecations="true" restrictNotices="true" restrictWarnings="true">
        <include>
            <directory>app</directory>
        </include>
    </source>

    <php>
        <env name="APP_ENV" value="testing"/>

        <!--
            APP_KEY, for the suite only. It is delivered as a FILE in every real environment
            (APP_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/app_key), and the arch and unit suites run with no secret
            mounts at all — so without this line every session-authenticated Feature test dies on
            MissingAppKeyException before it reaches the assertion it was written for.

            Deliberately NOT the KEK. APP_KEY covers sessions and cookies; KB_KEK wraps provider
            credentials. Sharing one value here would make the suite unable to detect the day
            somebody shares them in production, where "log everyone out" and "re-wrap every
            tenant's provider credential" would become the same operation.
        -->
        <env name="APP_KEY" value="base64:Y2Ftby10ZXN0LWFwcC1rZXktMzItYnl0ZXMtbG9uZyE="/>
        <env name="APP_MAINTENANCE_DRIVER" value="file"/>
        <env name="APP_DEBUG" value="true"/>

        <!--
            POSTGRESQL, NEVER SQLITE. The schema depends on jsonb, partial unique indexes (the
            single-active-version constraint that makes publication atomic), CHECK constraints, and
            the COMPOSITE foreign keys that guard bot_source_assignments — the one row that can span
            two organizations. SQLite silently accepts what PostgreSQL rejects, so a green suite on
            SQLite proves nothing about the constraint that is the entire point.

            Under parallel testing Laravel appends the worker token and creates kb_test_1 … kb_test_N
            automatically. It does that FOR THE DATABASE ONLY — the Qdrant collection, the Valkey
            logical databases and the cache prefix are token-scoped by hand in
            AppServiceProvider::isolateParallelTestWorkers(). (XML comments cannot contain a double
            hyphen, which is why no CLI flag is written out literally in this file.)

            THE HOST IS `postgres-test`, NOT `postgres`, AND THE SUFFIX IS THE POINT. The test
            database has to be a DIFFERENT NAME from the one the application runs against, not merely
            a different database on the same name: `postgres` is the dev and production service, and
            a suite that resolves it is one stale shell, one wrong Compose project, or one absent
            DB_DATABASE away from running RefreshDatabase's migrate:fresh against real tenant data.
            The failure is silent and total, and it is not recoverable from the test run.

            These four values are canonical and shared: infrastructure/docker/compose.yaml's `test`
            profile and the CI service container use the same host, database, user and password.
            Changing one of them here without changing them there does not fail loudly — it fails as
            "connection refused" in whichever environment was not updated, which reads like an
            infrastructure flake rather than a config edit.
        -->
        <env name="DB_CONNECTION" value="pgsql"/>
        <env name="DB_HOST" value="postgres-test"/>
        <env name="DB_PORT" value="5432"/>
        <env name="DB_DATABASE" value="kb_test"/>
        <env name="DB_USERNAME" value="kb_test"/>
        <env name="DB_PASSWORD" value="kb_test"/>

        <!--
            The default cache store stays on the NON-EVICTING Valkey instance even in tests, because
            WithoutOverlapping, ShouldBeUnique and the scheduler mutexes cannot be pointed anywhere
            else and every one of them fails OPEN when its key disappears. `array` would make a
            two-replica onOneServer test pass while proving the opposite of what it claims.
            Never call Cache::flush() or FLUSHDB in a test: under parallel testing it wipes the siblings.

            REDIS_HOST IS PINNED HERE FOR THE SAME REASON DB_HOST IS, AND THE HAZARD IS WORSE.
            config/database.php defaults it to `valkey-core`, which is the DEV AND PRODUCTION
            instance, so before this line an unpinned suite resolved the real one. That store is
            non-evicting and holds the scheduler mutexes, WithoutOverlapping and ShouldBeUnique
            locks — so a test run against it does not read stale data, it takes and drops the locks
            a running scheduler depends on, and every one of those fails OPEN when its key vanishes.
            `valkey-test` is the service name the Compose `test` profile already uses.

            REDIS_CACHE_HOST IS DELIBERATELY LEFT UNSET, so the `ephemeral` connection still
            resolves `valkey-cache` and fails with a connection error the first time a test reaches
            for it. Pointing it at valkey-test as well would put the EVICTING and NON-EVICTING
            keyspaces on one server at the same logical database, and a lock wrongly written to the
            evicting store would then pass every test — which is the bug the two-instance split
            exists to catch. A loud "connection refused" is the correct answer until the test
            profile grows a second Valkey.
        -->
        <env name="CACHE_STORE" value="valkey"/>
        <env name="REDIS_HOST" value="valkey-test"/>
        <env name="REDIS_CLIENT" value="phpredis"/>
        <env name="SESSION_DRIVER" value="array"/>

        <!--
            `sync` for the default run so a Feature test's dispatch executes inline. Integration/
            tests that exercise a real worker, a retry_after expiry, or a lock TTL must override
            this to `valkey` explicitly — asserting on a job payload instead is how a queue test
            stops testing anything.
        -->
        <env name="QUEUE_CONNECTION" value="sync"/>

        <env name="BCRYPT_ROUNDS" value="4"/>

        <!--
            Telemetry off in the suite: the PHP SDK has no background thread, so the batch processor
            flushes inline at shutdown and a missing Collector adds its full timeout to every test.
            OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT is absent here for the same reason
            it is absent from .env.example: it has no permitted value in any environment, including
            this one, because it captures user questions, assembled prompts and retrieved tenant
            chunks into spans.

            Note what does NOT stop it. The fail-closed boot check exists only in the data plane
            (services/ai-service/app/observability/otel.py raises when the name is present in the
            environment at all). There is no equivalent in this service, so adding the variable to
            this block would not fail the suite and nothing would say so — it would just start
            capturing. Writing that boot check is open work, not done work.
        -->
        <env name="OTEL_PHP_AUTOLOAD_ENABLED" value="false"/>
        <env name="OTEL_SDK_DISABLED" value="true"/>

        <!--
            THE KEY-ENCRYPTING KEY, FOR TESTS ONLY. Provider credentials are envelope-encrypted
            (kb-security-baseline §18.2) and CredentialVault fails CLOSED on a missing KEK — which
            is correct, and which means the fixture connection factory cannot seal anything
            without one. A literal here rather than a mounted file because the arch/unit suites
            run with no secret mounts at all, and because a suite that silently skipped encryption
            would make every redaction assertion vacuous.

            It is deliberately not a real-looking key: secret scanners flag those on every PR
            forever. It is also not APP_KEY — sharing one key would make "log everyone out" and
            "re-wrap every tenant's provider credential" the same operation.
        -->
        <env name="KB_KEK" value="kb-test-kek-not-a-real-key-0000000000000000"/>
        <env name="KB_KEK_VERSION" value="1"/>

        <!-- Deterministic fixtures. Real credentials never reach the suite. -->
        <env name="AI_SERVICE_URL" value="http://ai-api:8000"/>
        <env name="AI_HMAC_ACTIVE_KEY_ID" value="k1"/>
        <env name="AI_HMAC_KEY_K1" value="test-hmac-secret-k1"/>
        <env name="QDRANT_COLLECTION" value="kb_test"/>
    </php>
</phpunit>
